\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging debt languishes on Russian uncertainty. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hugh Bronstein \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 1998\par 00:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 9 (Reuters) - Trading in emerging debt was slow and directionless Thursday as investors looked to events in Russia but found little in the way of news to push the market one way or the other, traders and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment improved, but not by much, on news that Russian Deputy Prime Minister Viktor Khristenko said talks with the International Monetary Fund are going well and deals on new credits from both the IMF and the World Bank should be reached this week. \par \par \uc2  Market reaction was similarly muted when President Boris Yeltsin said Russia would not devalue the rouble, despite a crisis draining its reserves. \par \par \uc2  "Every headline moves the market a half point to one point up or down," one New York-based emerging sovereign debt trader said. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen these headlines before - that the IMF negotiations are going fine," the trader said. "This keeps the market from falling father but it does not bring confidence." \par \par \uc2  In order to take direction, the market needs concrete news about the size of Russia's IMF package and the conditions the IMF will impose as part of the deal, traders said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russia PRIN bonds\tab \tab   were up 1/2 to bid 45 and her IAN debt \tab     was up 5/8 to bid 52-1/2.\par Benchmark Brazil C bonds\tab \tab   were flat on the day at a bid of 74-7/8 and Argentina PAR bonds\tab \tab  were flat at a bid of 75-1/2.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Elsewhere, the dollar rose against the yen on Thursday amid growing scepticism that Japan's government would take sufficient measures to stimulate its economy, despite pledges of tax reform, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "All the fundamental economic information coming out of Asia right now is very negative," said Devi Aurora, Asia analyst at Standard & Poor's DRI. "A whole lot more needs to be done in terms of government policy in Japan before sentiment is turned around."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Until then, emerging market debt will continue to suffer - and the U.S. Treasury market will continue to benefit - from a flight to quality, she said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark U.S. Treasury bond was up 9/32 at 107-12/32 late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Luis R. Luis, head of emerging market bond research at Scudder Kemper Investments, said the yen, while weak, seems to have found a stable trading range. This, he said, accounts for the fact that the bonds of Asian countries have held their own in recent days.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One of the worst Brady bond performers of the day was Venezuela, whose GLB27 paper\tab \tab  slipped 1 to bid 71.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Duff & Phelps Credit Rating Co. on Thursday said it lowered its rating on Venezuela's bolivar-denominated public national debt (DPN) to CCC from BB because of a reported missed payment on principal and interest last week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The move was important, analysts said, because it downgraded Venezuelan local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt to default status.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are two ways for the Venezuelan government to defend the bolivar, direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by selling dollars or issuing Treasury and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  paper," said Siobhan Manning, Latin American debt analyst at Paine Webber.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If the latter is compromised by this downgrade, the government will bleed down reserves as it \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by spending dollars on bolivars," she said.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Venezuela\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had announced plans to sell $500 million in a global bond issue, to be priced when oil prices improve and spreads narrow. The downgrade can only hurt that effort, Manning said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (( Emerging Markets Unit, 212-859-1728 )).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7901a69\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
