\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuela Bolivar Closes Weaker Vs. Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 1998\par 22:07\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar closed weaker against the dollar Tuesday in active trading as businesses snapped up dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a midrate of 533.00 bolivars, up from Monday's close of 532.00. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 532.50/533.50 bolivars. Spreads of major banks ranged from 532.00/533.00 to 532.60/533.60. \par \par \uc2  Businesses, such as importers and manufacturers, were aggressively buying up dollars Tuesday, although the buying volume were well below last week's frenetic levels, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There's still a bit nervousness out there, but most businesses were buying simply because the exchange rate looks attractive," a trader said. "Most are expecting the bolivar to continue weakening in coming days." \par \par \uc2  Accumulated nervousness about the poor economic news, falling foreign reserves and the recent rise in the bolivar's rate of depreciation triggered a flurry of dollar-buying late last week. \par \par \uc2  When the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily last week in defense of the bolivar, it gave the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a respite from its steady weakening against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  With dollar-demand strong, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold large quantities of dollars to sate the market's dollar-demand, according to traders. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold roughly $60 million to $65 million during the entire day. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  typically sells roughly $40 million daily to the\par local market. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which by law must buy most of the\par dollars earned by state-owned oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA\par (PDVSA), is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market.\par    -By Greg Cancelada; 582-564-3291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  21-04-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2007GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010917du4l0039t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
