\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuela Bolivar Closes Stronger Vs. Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 265 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 1998\par 22:04\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar closed slightly stronger against the dollar Friday in heavy trading as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local market by dumping dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a midrate of 531.25 bolivars, down from Thursday's close of 531.50. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 530.75/531.75 bolivars. Spreads of major banks ranged from 529.75/530.75 to 531.60/532.60. \par \par \uc2  For the second consecutive day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sold off large quantities of dollars to the local foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in an aggressive defense of the bolivar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting selling dollars as soon as the market opened. This helped calm down the market, though there was still a lot of dollar-demand out there," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  After opening at around 532.50 bolivars, the dollar slowly lost ground on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-selling. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $100 million to $120 million during the entire session Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally sells roughly $40 million daily to the market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by state-owned oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market. \par \par \uc2  Accumulated nervousness about the poor economic news, falling international reserves and the recent rise in the bolivar's rate of depreciation has spurred dollar-buying in the past two days. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  17-04-98 \par \par \uc2  2004GMT \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010917du4h0034z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
