\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -4:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkt Thu. \b0\par\par\uc2 269 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 1998\par 01:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vice-president, however, gave no indications as to how much further reserves are expected to fall. \par \par \uc2  Romero also gave no indication to what is the minimum reserve level that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted to hold. \par \par \uc2  'We will hold (enough) foreign reserves sufficient to maintaining the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  band,' he said. \par \par \uc2  Romero admitted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been forced to sell a large amount of dollars Thursday to the local foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in order to 'satisfy' the market's dollar-demand. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, a spurt of heavy dollar-buying by a nervous local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell off dollars in defense of the bolivar. \par \par \uc2  After running up to 534.50 bolivars in the late morning, the dollar finished Thursday at 531.50 bolivars due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar closed Wednesday at 531.00 bolivars. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold roughly $150 million during Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically sells around $40 million daily to the local market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by state-owned oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market. \par \par \uc2  Romero noted that the recent rise in dollar-demand has been due to short-lived, 'speculative attacks' rather than a rise in 'normal' national demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite such speculative attacks, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would maintain its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band, he reiterated.\par    -By Greg Cancelada; 582-564-3291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-04-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2301GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4g037gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
