\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuela Bolivar Closes Weaker Vs. Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1998\par 21:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar closed slightly weaker against the dollar Thursday in heavy trading, recovering most of its earlier losses after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at midday and dumped dollars in the market, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a midrate of 531.50 bolivars, up from Wednesday's close of 531.00. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 531.00/532.00 bolivars. Spreads of major banks ranged from 530.75/531.75 to 533.00/534.00. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar sustained a hard tumble Thursday morning, pushed lower by heavy dollar-buying on nervousness due to recent unfavorable news and worry about the recent speed of the bolivar's depreciation. \par \par \uc2  Some traders reported the dollar as high as 536.50 bolivars in the late morning as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to sell many dollars to the market. \par \par \uc2  By 1500 GMT, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sold off huge sums of dollars, which quickly brought the bolivar to finish the day only slightly weaker, traders said. \par \par \uc2  'Once the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began to defend the bolivar, it calmed down the dollar-buying frenzy,' a trader said. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold roughly $150 million during the entire session. Normally, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells around $40 million in a single session. \par \par \uc2  Nervousness has been exacerbated in recent days given the apparent change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to defend a specific rate of depreciation for the bolivar. \par \par \uc2  After months of keeping the depreciation closely tracked to its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  band 'slide' of 1.28%, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has allowed the bolivar to depreciate at a faster rate in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by\par state-owned oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the\par biggest supplier of dollars to the market.\par    -By Greg Cancelada; 582-564-3291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-04-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1922GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4g03758\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
