\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuela Bolivar Strengthens Vs. Dollar At Midday \b0\par\par\uc2 215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 16:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar strengthened slightly against the dollar midday Tuesday as Venezuelan corporations continued to hoard bolivars ahead of a March 31 tax deadline, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At 1545 GMT, the dollar was quoted at a midrate of 522.00 bolivars, down from Monday's close of 523.00 as demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dried up. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 521.50/522.50 bolivars to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  'There is a scarcity of bolivars on the market at the moment. The financial system has good underlying liquidity, but it is being affected by companies' need to pay taxes,' commented one trader. \par \par \uc2  She added that the financial system might expect to see bolivar liquidity tighten until next Tuesday, when the deadline for tax payments passes. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to a minimum, given the scarcity of dollar-demand, and sold only an estimated $20 to $25 million during the morning. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by\par state-run oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the biggest\par supplier of dollars to the market.\par -By Daniel Flynn; 582-564-3291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 24-03-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1549GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du3o02ily\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\