\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuela Bolivar Closes Weaker Vs. Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 1998\par 19:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--The bolivar closed slightly weaker against the dollar Thursday in active trading despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by heavily selling dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a midrate of 515.50 bolivars, up from Wednesday's close of 515.25 bolivars. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 515.00/516.00 bolivars. Spreads of major banks ranged from 515.00/516.00 to 515.50/516.50. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that corporations continued to snap up dollars, spurred by the expectation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be able to absorb a large spurt of liquidity that will hit the financial system on Friday. \par \par \uc2  With more than 200 billion bolivars in principal and interest on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  paper - TEM bills and CDs - to be paid out on Friday, traders expect a large portion of this will be going straight to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling certificates of deposit to the market in order to drain liquidity, but has only been able to see a total of 102.8 billion bolivars in CDs this week. \par \par \uc2  'The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't announced its CD auction size for Friday, so some (market participants) are worried about how the bolivar will fare given the rise in liquidity,' a trader said. \par \par \uc2  After staying on the sidelines most of the morning, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  returned to the market with a vengence in the afternoon, according to traders. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $80 million to $120 million during the course of the day, with the bulk sold in the last hour of trading. On a normal day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells around $40 million. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, traders said news that Moody's Investors Service has grown more pessimistic on the country didn't have a strong impact on the market. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Moody's revised its outlook on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt to negative from stable due to the government's inability to respond effectively to the country. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 'Most dollar-buyers were responding Thursday to the threat of rising\par liquidity rather than Moody's,' a trader said.\par    -By Greg Cancelada; 582-564-3291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 12-02-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1821GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du2c017r6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
