\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela \highlight\uc2  Bolivar Ends Down;  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1998\par 22:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (Dow Jones)--Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in Wednesday's trading session reduced the bolivar's losses on the day against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a midrate of 505.00 bolivars, up from Tuesday's close of 504.75 bolivars. \par \par \uc2  The average bank spread was 504.50/505.50. Spreads of major banks ranged from 504.50/505.50 to 504.90/505.90. \par \par \uc2  As was the case Tuesday, rock-bottom domestic interest rates had banks and other investors buying up dollars throughout the day in search of higher returns abroad. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the dollar had gained all the way to a midrate of around 505.30 bolivars as of 1950 GMT, some ten minutes before the close of the session, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to enter the market and aggressively sell dollars to bring support to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unloaded over $100 million in that span of ten minutes. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which by law must buy most of the dollars earned by state-run oil monopoly Petroleos de Venezuela SA (PDVSA), is the biggest supplier of dollars to the market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though, normally sells around $40 million a day. \par \par \uc2  'The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market aggressively and managed to push the dollar back down some,' one trader said. \par \par \uc2  High liquidity levels in the local financial system - the result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent loose monetary policy - have short term domestic interest rates at extremely low levels. \par \par \uc2  The overnight, interbank rate closed Wednesday at 4.5%, up slightly from Tuesday's close of 3%-4%. \par \par \uc2  'There is just so much cash in the system right now, and a lot of it, logically, is chasing dollars,' one trader said. \par \par \uc2  The recent drop in international oil prices has also began to create some concern in the market that petroleum-exporting Venezuela's current account surplus will take a bigger hit this year than previously expected. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A weaker current account situation could lead to a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par down the road, and this is pushing some investors into dollars as well.\par -By David Papadopoulos; 582-564-3291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-01-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2102GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1155 1864 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du17004sk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
