\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela \highlight\uc2  bolivar ends soft, Cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CARACAS, Aug 20 (Reuter) - The Venezuelan \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  waded into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday as investors dropped the bolivar following a steep drop in local interest rates, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar closed off at 495.00/496.00 per dollar in busy and turbulent trade, marginally softer than Tuesday's 494.75/495.75 close. But traders said the unit was only brought back from 497 per dollar by strong \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar sales towards the end of the day. \par \par \uc2  "The bank had to come in heavily, injecting a lot of dollars to bring the exchange rate down to its close," said one. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the bolivar sell-off was sparked by a 307 basis points slump in six month TEM yields to 18.82 percent in the Bank's regular weekly TEM auction. Six month TEM paper is used as a benchmark for wider interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Even though the (TEM yield) drop was expected by the market, it combined with strong corporate demand to weaken the exchange rate," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  ((-- Ana Isabel Martinez, Caracas newsroom, 582 505 2600)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8k00wlz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
