\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela \highlight\uc2 's Bolivar Loses Vs Dlr;  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1996\par 22:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (AP-Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bolivar closed weaker against the dollar Tuesday, but managed during the later session to retrace some of its losses in early trading with help from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at a midrate of 472.00 bolivars, up from 471.875 bolivars Friday, but down from 473.00 bolivars at the close of the early session. Monday was a market holiday. \par \par \uc2  The average spread in the market was 471.50-472.50 bolivars. Banks quoted spreads ranging from 471.00-472.25 bolivars to 472.75-473.50 bolivars. \par \par \uc2  Corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  started early and continued into the late session, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling dollars to meet demand at about 1830 GMT and then again around 1930 GMT before the market closed at 2000 GMT, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in when the dollar was around 473.00 to 473.50 bolivars. \par \par \uc2  There were mixed opinions about whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market in the morning, with some traders dismissing the idea and others sure that it sold dollars near the end of early trading at 1130 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said much of the demand came from one particular bank, while others said the demand for dollars was spread among numerous corporate clients. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also split over what course the bolivar would take Wednesday. Some saw it continuing to lose ground as demand for dollars continued. But others said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's activity Tuesday would pacify buyers who fear dollars will grow more expensive. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 11-06-96 \par \par \uc2  2046GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds6b04skv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
