\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuela Money Markets Remain Liquid, Mostly Quiet \b0\par\par\uc2 219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 1996\par 23:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (AP-Dow Jones)--Venezuela's money markets remained extremely liquid Friday, with overnight, interbank lending rates closing the week between 10% and 12%, traders said. \par \par \uc2  With interest rate controls eliminated last month, banks have raised their lending rates to prohibitive levels and have not moved their deposit rates up in stride. That has left banks with large amounts of cash on hand as loan demand has dropped, traders said. Also, depositors prefer to invest money in the stock market or buy dollars instead of keeping their cash in the bank, some traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela sold 40-day treasury bills at a yield of around 37%, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to defend the bolivar, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said. \par \par \uc2  But money market traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't sell monetary stabilization (TEM) bills or certificates of deposits, a tactic it has used in the past to encourage investors to buy bolivars. \par \par \uc2  On the secondary TEM market, 88-day bills were changing hands at a yield of around 41%, down from 43.33% at Wednesday's auction, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Money markets are closed Monday for a bank holiday. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 17-05-96 \par \par \uc2  2150GMT \par \par \uc2  1155 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds5h043sz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pn