\par\par \b \uc2  Venezuela Bolivar Gains Ground Vs Dlr As Sellers Abound \b0\par\par\uc2 226 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1996\par 18:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CARACAS (AP-Dow Jones)--The bolivar gained ground Tuesday against the dollar during early trading in Caracas, where many corporations and institutional players were selling dollars, according to traders. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at the close of the early session at 1530 GMT at a bid/offer of 485-486 bolivars, up from Monday's close of 499-500 bolivars. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the dollar started trading around 501 bolivars, but the Venezuelan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadily strengthened as sellers came into the market. \par \par \uc2  The rate is 'super attractive' for companies who are looking to pay off debts in bolivars and who are long in dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Another trader said part of the bolivar's strength came from a single bank that was selling a large supply of dollars. \par \par \uc2  'Apparently, there was a bank in the market making a strong offer,' the trader said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Venezuela\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders were split over where the later trading session would lead the bolivar. Some said the earlier trend could continue, with dollar sellers still strong. Others said the exchange rate was low enough to attract buyers. \par \par \uc2  'It could generate demand,' one trader said. 'It should go up a bit. People might want to take positions now.' \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 23-04-96 \par \par \uc2  1634GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds4n03g2j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex10